1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,178
Food apps don't solve indecision.

2
00:00:02,178 --> 00:00:03,428
They multiply it.

3
00:00:03,428 --> 00:00:05,730
OneDish takes the opposite approach.

4
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:09,368
Tell it what today looks like - or don't - and tap once.

5
00:00:09,368 --> 00:00:10,309
No feed.

6
00:00:10,309 --> 00:00:11,522
No swiping.

7
00:00:11,522 --> 00:00:12,648
Just one meal.

8
00:00:13,396 --> 00:00:19,132
Optional context can sharpen the choice: budget, time, allergies, cravings, and

9
00:00:19,132 --> 00:00:20,696
eventually wellness signals.

10
00:00:20,696 --> 00:00:22,871
But the default remains one tap.

11
00:00:22,871 --> 00:00:28,746
Missing information stays unknown, and hard allergy rules are never relaxed.

12
00:00:29,352 --> 00:00:33,382
Behind that tap, OneDish starts with ninety demo dishes.

13
00:00:33,382 --> 00:00:37,684
The decision is already complete before this animation begins.

14
00:00:37,684 --> 00:00:40,049
You're not watching fake A.I. thinking.

15
00:00:40,049 --> 00:00:45,074
You're watching the evidence: availability, safety, nutrition, repetition, taste, time,

16
00:00:45,074 --> 00:00:49,596
and budget - reduced, step by step, to one.

17
00:00:50,404 --> 00:00:51,529
Here's the answer.

18
00:00:51,529 --> 00:00:56,866
One dish, with a price estimate, calorie and protein ranges, and the exact reasons it

19
00:00:56,866 --> 00:00:57,222
won.

20
00:00:57,222 --> 00:01:03,250
The menu is clearly labeled as demo data, and every decision keeps its input hash.

21
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:09,190
If this misses, I can give one reason and get one reserve - not another endless list.

22
00:01:09,892 --> 00:01:12,067
That correction stays on this device.

23
00:01:12,067 --> 00:01:17,317
Over time, Taste Orbit turns meal history into visible preference memory.

24
00:01:17,317 --> 00:01:23,326
I can focus a signal, inspect why it matters, then tap You and return to the natural

25
00:01:23,326 --> 00:01:23,679
orbit.

26
00:01:23,679 --> 00:01:27,117
It's useful, but it never becomes a hidden black box.

27
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:30,472
Privacy is a boundary you can see.

28
00:01:30,472 --> 00:01:36,977
Meal history, taste, identifiers, health signals, and precise location each have a clear

29
00:01:36,977 --> 00:01:39,980
purpose, storage rule, and deletion rule.

30
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:42,808
Health data does not leave the device.

31
00:01:42,808 --> 00:01:48,555
Location only reaches OpenStreetMap after permission, and the connector shows that path

32
00:01:48,555 --> 00:01:49,992
- nothing more.

33
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:56,639
OneDish is an offline-ready P.W.A. with deterministic decisions and optional A.I.

34
00:01:56,639 --> 00:01:59,298
interpretation behind a strict boundary.

35
00:01:59,298 --> 00:02:04,030
It doesn't order for you, and it doesn't pretend demo inventory is live.

36
00:02:04,030 --> 00:02:07,997
It does one thing well: stop browsing, and eat this.
